{
  "_readme": [
    "Ground truth for the security benchmark. Four sections, and the second is",
    "the one that matters: `planted` findings measure recall, `decoys` measure",
    "precision. A scanner that fires on everything scores perfect recall, so",
    "recall alone proves nothing.",
    "",
    "A planted entry is matched by (file, category) with the line within",
    "`line_tolerance`. `rule_any` lists acceptable rule ids where more than one",
    "detector legitimately catches the same thing; omit it to accept any rule.",
    "`expect_severity` is the floor the finding must be reported at — a real",
    "credential ranked 'low' is nearly as useless as a missed one.",
    "",
    "A decoy is a location that must produce NO finding of that category. Each",
    "one is a real false-positive mode observed in actual secret scanners and",
    "linters, not an invented edge case. Two of them are known to fire; they are",
    "marked `known_failure` with the reason, so the score stays honest instead",
    "of the fixture being softened until it passes.",
    "",
    "`downgraded` covers the third case: findings that must appear, but quietly.",
    "A high-entropy value in a documentation file is usually an illustration, and",
    "ranking it alongside a live credential is what trains people to ignore",
    "secret findings."
  ],
  "line_tolerance": 2,

